00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:38.450
But in fact, let's just
kind of jump right in.

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:40.870
What did that implementation
process look like for you,

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:42.870
in terms of getting Chrome
into your environment

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:44.370
and into the hands
of your end users?

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:45.203
JASON TAHANEY: Sure.

00:06:45.203 --> 00:06:46.950
So we started with
a proof of concept.

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:50.080
And I mean, the important things
like I mentioned were security,

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:51.503
it had to be easy to use.

00:06:51.503 --> 00:06:52.920
We also were looking
for some cost

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:53.855
savings in there
too, because that

00:06:53.855 --> 00:06:55.510
was kind of the promise of it.

00:06:55.510 --> 00:06:58.800
And on the security
front, the promise

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:00.610
has worked out very well.

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:02.600
I mean, you needed
expensive software

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:05.370
in our Windows environment
to deploy encryption,

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:06.630
to take one example.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:10.110
And the fact that it comes by
default in our Chromebooks.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:14.070
And also tying to that, patching
and kind of the standard things

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:17.040
you need to do in the Windows
environment, the Chrome devices

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:18.878
auto patch and do a
very nice job of that.

00:07:18.878 --> 00:07:19.920
We really don't have to--

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:21.795
I don't want to say it's
set it and forget it

00:07:21.795 --> 00:07:24.060
kind of-- in security you
never want to do that.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:27.023
But it's giving us a
good sense of confidence

00:07:27.023 --> 00:07:28.190
during the proof of concept.

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:30.023
DIANA CHONG: Do you
happen to remember or do

00:07:30.023 --> 00:07:31.860
you have a sense
of how long it took

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:33.240
for you to kind of prove it out.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:35.710
How long did POC take for
you to really make that go,

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:36.912
no go decision?

00:07:36.912 --> 00:07:38.620
JASON TAHANEY: We did
about three months.

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:40.912
But just because we were
doing the security part of it.

00:07:40.912 --> 00:07:44.070
We wanted to integrate it
into our Microsoft environment

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:45.280
as well for authentication.

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:47.200
So we want to make sure users
are using the same username

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:49.408
and password that they used
to log into their Windows

00:07:49.408 --> 00:07:51.990
computer-- make sure that
worked on Chrome devices.

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:55.620
And so that part of
it worked very well.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:08:00.507
Also the ease of use and want to
understand-- we kind of put it

00:08:00.507 --> 00:08:02.590
out to some of our clinicians
to kind of see, hey,

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:03.260
what do you think of this?

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:04.380
It is going to work pretty well?

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:06.047
And we actually created
some power users

00:08:06.047 --> 00:08:07.430
to help us with that process.

00:08:07.430 --> 00:08:10.160
And we got really good feedback.

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:13.140
I would say that we did a
lot of training and education

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:14.982
in the beginning,
but I would say

00:08:14.982 --> 00:08:16.440
I don't believe
you really need it.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:19.320
I think I heard the number
is about 1.5 billion Gmail

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:21.720
users today.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.280
And these are Chrome devices,
but having that familiarity,

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:25.590
it's very similar.

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:27.120
Google kind of
keeps their feel--

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:29.650
they keep their products and
the look and feel very similar.

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:31.442
And I think that's
carried over into Chrome

00:08:31.442 --> 00:08:33.461
and it's worked out pretty well.

00:08:33.461 --> 00:08:34.919
DIANA CHONG: And
Josh, maybe we can

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:37.789
talk a little bit about your
implementation process as well.

00:08:37.789 --> 00:08:39.780
And what were some
of the challenges

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:42.720
that you experienced while
you're piloting it and then

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:43.928
rolling it out to the stores?

00:08:43.928 --> 00:08:45.428
JOSHUA CHADD: Yeah,
I would probably

00:08:45.428 --> 00:08:47.340
say the biggest
challenges we had

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:51.120
were the logistics
of just shipping

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:55.920
a device from a central
location to about

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:01.920
10,000 different stores that
are geographically diverse

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:06.150
and without having to do a
lot of pre-configuration,

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:09.730
as well as implementing the
security that we needed.

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:14.640
So we had to partner
with Google Agosto

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:19.230
to write a custom app for us
for an out of box experience.

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:22.170
So when it did actually
get to a store,

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:26.580
they could go through a
very short set up process

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:27.420
so that we could--

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:29.160
on our side in IT--

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:33.780
we could identify
where that device is.

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:36.060
So if the store
calls in, we don't

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:38.730
need them to read off a serial
number or something like that.

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:40.560
We can just look
up their location

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.150
and find the affected device.

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:47.580
And the other piece was
getting client certificates

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:50.440
on the device as
easy as possible.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:52.980
And with that, we also
used product Cloud Path

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:55.260
to integrate in with
our PKI so that we

00:09:55.260 --> 00:10:00.510
could during the enrollment
process provision certificates

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:04.320
onto that device for things like
Wi-Fi access and client cert

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:06.843
auth for certain internal sites.

00:10:06.843 --> 00:10:08.260
DIANA CHONG: And
I know I remember

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:10.460
when we were doing the
deployment initially,

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:12.680
there were a bunch of
security considerations

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:16.452
that you guys had to
account for with Starbucks.

00:10:16.452 --> 00:10:18.660
Maybe you can speak a little
bit about that as well--

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:19.910
the challenges there.

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:24.950
JOSHUA CHADD: Yeah, so one thing
that we really were careful of

00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:27.630
is we wanted this to
be a shared device.

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:30.680
So in a store of anywhere
from 10 to 100 partners

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:35.000
that work in there, that no
data is going to stay resident

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:37.040
on the device after
the session is done,

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:39.680
as well as there is no
data from a store manager

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:43.520
that's accessible by a
barista or someone else

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:45.080
that they don't need to see.

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:47.000
So that was a huge
consideration for us.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:49.670
So we actually run
our Chromebooks

00:10:49.670 --> 00:10:54.890
in kind of an ephemeral mode,
where every time they log off,

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:56.570
every single thing
about that session

00:10:56.570 --> 00:10:58.580
is wiped from the computer.

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:04.610
So it maintains security and a
security posture, and also just

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:07.370
a legal compliance posture
that works really well

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:09.191
for our company.

00:11:09.191 --> 00:11:10.790
DIANA CHONG: OK, thank you.

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:12.922
And Donna, your turn.

00:11:12.922 --> 00:11:14.380
We'll talk a little
bit-- now we're

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:16.630
moving into more of the
changed management front.

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:20.230
And Donna definitely
led a huge effort there

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:21.892
for her particular
implementation.

00:11:21.892 --> 00:11:23.350
So maybe you can
speak a little bit

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:25.690
about what's required
from a change management

00:11:25.690 --> 00:11:28.820
perspective for Chrome
and a bit of G Suite

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:30.760
since they're all tied together.

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:32.100
DONNA CHORNAWKA: For sure.

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:37.888
We went live in July 2017
and we have over 5,500

00:11:37.888 --> 00:11:40.180
team members-- that's what
we call our employees-- that

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:44.920
are located across the
province of Alberta in Canada

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:47.980
and we have over 300 locations.

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:49.780
And so for us as
we looked at how

00:11:49.780 --> 00:11:52.190
to introduce Chrome
and implement

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:55.480
G Suite across the province, and
to help all of our team members

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:59.170
through the change, learning
both the G Suite toolset

00:11:59.170 --> 00:12:02.440
but then also accessing
Chrome browser,

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:04.090
we really took a
different look at it

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:05.560
and how we can help our folks.

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:10.600
And so really for us the three
success key factors for that

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:13.900
was that we went out and
we seconded 50 people

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:18.520
from across our province, who
we then called G Evangelists.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:22.360
And what they were were really
folks that were very diverse.

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:24.490
They were from all
regions of our province,

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:28.180
so it wasn't just the big urban
cities, it was from rural areas

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:29.210
as well.

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:32.080
They were also selected
from people from--

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:34.910
as we are a financial
institution--

00:12:34.910 --> 00:12:37.870
we had people from
our tellers, our CSRs,

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:40.990
all the way up to managing
directors in corporate office.

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:43.120
And what that did is
really bring together

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:47.410
a diverse group of 50 people who
really we trained in a 30 day

00:12:47.410 --> 00:12:51.880
boot camp on G Suite,
on Chrome, on how

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:53.380
to help our team members.

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:56.650
And really it was about
listening to our team members

00:12:56.650 --> 00:12:58.630
and helping them
through the change.

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:04.090
Our key mantra was no one
was going to be left behind.

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:08.350
Because we're an
80-year-old startup company,

00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:10.600
and it's really
about understanding

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:13.600
that we had people who have just
started with a company when we

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:15.610
went live and we
also had people who

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:20.670
had been in our financial
institution for over 30 years,

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:21.170
right.

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:24.970
And so it's really understanding
that team members and people

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:27.760
all go through their
own change management

00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:29.540
curve in a different way.

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:33.130
And so this was really key
for us to be successful.

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:36.460
As we went through the
change the following year,

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:39.680
we then introduced Pixelbooks
into our environment.

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:41.800
And so I would say the
second success factor

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:45.550
there was we really focused on
our C Suite and our executives

00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:46.480
first.

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:48.280
And you might
think, wow, that was

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:51.190
pretty bold to have
them test your tools,

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:53.170
and was that a lot
of handholding?

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:55.210
And I would say it wasn't.

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:58.930
They really found
the tool easy to use.

00:13:58.930 --> 00:14:01.750
And we thought that if
they would be onside,

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:03.700
that they would be our
biggest cheerleaders.

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:04.690
And they were.

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:07.640
So it really became that
they came to us and said,

00:14:07.640 --> 00:14:09.340
we want our teams to have this.

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:11.510
We want our leaders to have it.

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:16.360
And so right now most of our
users are C Suite or executive,

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:20.920
and for sure the branch managers
located across our province.

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:22.780
And then the third
key success factor

00:14:22.780 --> 00:14:27.340
I would say is last
year we brought in-house

00:14:27.340 --> 00:14:30.940
all of our technical
supports or IT help desk.

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:33.700
And so what we did is
we really grew a team

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:36.130
of who we called tech gurus.

00:14:36.130 --> 00:14:39.460
So we don't call them IT help
desk, we call them tech gurus.

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:41.830
And they're really
customer service oriented.

00:14:41.830 --> 00:14:46.030
And so they're really there to
help all of our team members

00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:49.840
with Chrome, their questions,
as well as Pixelbooks.

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:52.930
And we utilize-- you kind
of heard this morning

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:53.980
on the keynote speaker--

00:14:53.980 --> 00:14:57.730
we really utilize chat for
our team members to come in

00:14:57.730 --> 00:14:58.620
and ask questions.

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:02.020
So we have chat rooms set up, so
you can access our tech gurus.

00:15:02.020 --> 00:15:04.150
We have over 3,000
people already

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:07.910
in our tech chat room asking
questions about how to do this,

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:09.620
how to do that.

00:15:09.620 --> 00:15:11.800
And then the second
part of that group,

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:15.640
we also have product managers
who provide support on G Suite.

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:18.620
And so we have a Google G
Suite chat room as well.

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:20.800
And so between those
two forums, we're

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:24.910
able to have a group of
our own ATB team members

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:27.888
helping ATB team member's
with their technology.

00:15:27.888 --> 00:15:28.680
DIANA CHONG: Great.

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:34.420
Would you do anything different?

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:36.940
If you were to do this again,
would you do both together

00:15:36.940 --> 00:15:39.760
again, G Suite and Chrome?

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:43.460
Were there any challenges to
doing them so close together?

00:15:43.460 --> 00:15:47.110
DONNA CHORNAWKA: Yeah, we would
definitely do it together.

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:50.050
Because as they were learning
how to access G Suite,

00:15:50.050 --> 00:15:54.640
it just came in really easy
to then introduce Chrome.

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:56.905
So we would definitely
do that over again.

00:15:56.905 --> 00:15:58.280
JASON TAHANEY:
And I would agree.

00:15:58.280 --> 00:15:59.680
I would agree with that.

00:15:59.680 --> 00:16:02.350
Maybe deploy more during G
Suite, because it really was--

00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:04.870
we recognized it was good
value to doing the education,

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:07.350
but we didn't quite
totally get it

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:09.180
and it took a little
bit longer to deploy.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:11.810
So I definitely would deploy
more when you're doing G Suite

00:16:11.810 --> 00:16:14.900
and moving into Chrome.

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:17.990
DIANA CHONG: And now
let's talk about the aha

00:16:17.990 --> 00:16:19.070
moment for you guys.

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:21.380
When did you guys decide
Chrome was the right way

00:16:21.380 --> 00:16:23.030
to go, in terms
of the technology

00:16:23.030 --> 00:16:25.000
that you want to
move your work to.

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:27.253
Maybe we'll just start down
the row again from Josh.

00:16:27.253 --> 00:16:28.670
JOSHUA CHADD: Yeah,
I think for us

00:16:28.670 --> 00:16:32.240
it was really during
the pilot phase

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:34.970
when we were seeing
kind of the issues

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:38.950
that our pilot stories
were calling in about

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:42.200
and how simple
they were to solve.

00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:44.930
Because we eliminated a
lot of points of failure

00:16:44.930 --> 00:16:50.190
over a more traditional
kind of a Windows device

00:16:50.190 --> 00:16:51.930
that we'd had previously.

00:16:51.930 --> 00:16:54.710
So really there were
just very few things

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:57.890
that we had to spend
time troubleshooting.

00:16:57.890 --> 00:17:01.010
And it freed up a lot more
time for our help desk analysts

00:17:01.010 --> 00:17:04.579
to work on much more complex
calls, because they could solve

00:17:04.579 --> 00:17:06.980
things much more easily
or the stores themselves

00:17:06.980 --> 00:17:10.430
could solve it without
ever having to call in.

00:17:10.430 --> 00:17:14.000
DIANA CHONG: And I think when we
talked about it before you were

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:16.760
saying that there were
fewer calls coming in

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:18.099
from Chrome specifically.

00:17:18.099 --> 00:17:21.057
And now it's just basically
you managing everything

00:17:21.057 --> 00:17:22.338
coming in for Chrome.

00:17:22.338 --> 00:17:26.869
JOSHUA CHADD: Yeah, It's pretty
much today we're in, I think,

00:17:26.869 --> 00:17:30.170
year four of our deployment.

00:17:30.170 --> 00:17:32.280
And I'm the primary engineer.

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:34.250
And there are a couple
of analysts on my team

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:36.740
that also help support it,
as well as our help desk

00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:37.430
operation.

00:17:37.430 --> 00:17:41.000
But we have very few calls,
very few things escalated to us.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:45.650
It's probably less
than 5% of the work

00:17:45.650 --> 00:17:48.630
that we have come in today,
just to do any kind of support

00:17:48.630 --> 00:17:50.110
activities around them.

00:17:50.110 --> 00:17:51.230
DIANA CHONG: That's great.

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:54.250
And Donna, how about your
aha moment with Chrome?

00:17:54.250 --> 00:17:57.830
DONNA CHORNAWKA: Yeah, because
we introduced it with G Suite,

00:17:57.830 --> 00:18:00.740
the aha moment
came just as we saw

00:18:00.740 --> 00:18:04.520
people utilizing G
Suite in a much more

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:06.170
collaborative fashion.

00:18:06.170 --> 00:18:08.090
And so my aha
moment really came,

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:10.670
we also introduced
and put in CFMs

00:18:10.670 --> 00:18:13.620
in all of our meeting
rooms at the same time,

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:15.740
and so my aha
moment was just how

00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:17.570
easy it was to take
in your Chromebook

00:18:17.570 --> 00:18:20.330
or your Pixelbook into
one of our meeting rooms,

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:23.030
have the CFM right
there and you just

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:24.980
connect to that to the meeting.

00:18:24.980 --> 00:18:31.790
And so just the ecosystem
with having CFMs, Pixelbooks,

00:18:31.790 --> 00:18:34.640
and G Suite altogether
was my aha moment,

00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:38.660
by saying this is a great, easy
experience for team members

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:39.300
to use.

00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:40.780
DIANA CHONG: That's great.

00:18:40.780 --> 00:18:43.290
JASON TAHANEY: And for us,
I think there was a couple.

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:45.140
One was really when
our departments were

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:46.940
coming to us for Chromebooks.

00:18:46.940 --> 00:18:48.690
They had seen them,
other people had them,

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:50.690
and they were just coming
to us for Chromebooks,

00:18:50.690 --> 00:18:53.070
as well as wanting to use
them with G Suite application.

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:55.640
So one example was our
patient safety huddles,

00:18:55.640 --> 00:19:00.170
leadership meets daily to kind
of review any potential safety

00:19:00.170 --> 00:19:01.770
hazards in the organization.

00:19:01.770 --> 00:19:04.982
And we were using Chromebooks
for that and Google Sheets, so

00:19:04.982 --> 00:19:06.440
those two products
to kind of track

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:09.242
what we're doing
around patient safety.

00:19:09.242 --> 00:19:10.700
And it came to us,
right, we didn't

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:12.380
have to sell it, do
anything like that.

00:19:12.380 --> 00:19:15.440
And another example was in
our emergency department,

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:17.810
they actually developed
their own internet site

00:19:17.810 --> 00:19:21.380
with Google Sites
on Chrome devices.

00:19:21.380 --> 00:19:24.290
Because we were a large
Microsoft SharePoint customer

00:19:24.290 --> 00:19:27.170
prior to this, so
moving to Team Drive

00:19:27.170 --> 00:19:29.450
and the suite of
products with Chrome

00:19:29.450 --> 00:19:31.280
was probably one
of our goals was

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:33.590
to consolidate all that
data that we had out there.

00:19:33.590 --> 00:19:35.393
And so we were
driving that project,

00:19:35.393 --> 00:19:37.310
but then we also had
departments coming to us,

00:19:37.310 --> 00:19:39.290
like the ED, where they
wanted to do their own thing.

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:40.310
So that was pretty nice.

00:19:40.310 --> 00:19:43.440
And then IT, I would just
agree on the management side.

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:46.220
It's just incredibly easy
to manage, especially

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:47.180
on the security side.

00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:49.880
Whenever you hear
security is easy,

00:19:49.880 --> 00:19:51.530
you kind of question
that a little bit.

00:19:51.530 --> 00:19:53.685
But it truly has been for us.

00:19:53.685 --> 00:19:55.310
We have standardized
on Chrome browsers

00:19:55.310 --> 00:19:57.643
as well, and then the Chrome
devices that are out there.

00:19:57.643 --> 00:20:00.220
Seeing that auto patching work
and running a report and see,

00:20:00.220 --> 00:20:01.130
is this actually really working?

00:20:01.130 --> 00:20:02.260
And seeing that it is.

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:04.760
And by the way, we're doing
it without any extra software

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:07.010
so it's included-- it's
part of the platform, which

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:07.610
is very nice.

00:20:07.610 --> 00:20:09.980
And you get these
features by default.

00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:11.750
And they're
constantly innovating.

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:13.730
I mean, that's one
of our main reasons

00:20:13.730 --> 00:20:16.170
why we went with Google was
because of their ability

00:20:16.170 --> 00:20:16.670
to innovate.

00:20:16.670 --> 00:20:18.740
And we felt they
were the best vendor

00:20:18.740 --> 00:20:21.390
to do that as a cloud provider.

00:20:21.390 --> 00:20:23.280
DIANA CHONG: And so
what's next for you--

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:24.427
for all of you, I guess--

00:20:24.427 --> 00:20:25.760
in terms of your Chrome journey?

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:28.010
Where are you going
with adoption?

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:29.745
Are you rolling
out more devices?

00:20:29.745 --> 00:20:31.370
JASON TAHANEY: Well,
definitely for us,

00:20:31.370 --> 00:20:32.930
we're going to roll
out more devices.

00:20:32.930 --> 00:20:36.280
We have the partnership
with Health Cast and Google,

00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:38.480
that's really any day now.

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:40.208
And Health Cast is a
tap and go solution.

00:20:40.208 --> 00:20:41.750
So that's kind of
big in health care,

00:20:41.750 --> 00:20:43.583
being able to move from
computer to computer

00:20:43.583 --> 00:20:46.230
and have your desktop
session move with you via tap

00:20:46.230 --> 00:20:48.320
and go via an employee badge.

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:50.533
So once that is certified
to run on Chrome, which

00:20:50.533 --> 00:20:52.700
is coming any day, we're
going to be moving that out

00:20:52.700 --> 00:20:55.190
to all our clinicians
on anywhere

00:20:55.190 --> 00:20:56.600
we provide patient care.

00:20:56.600 --> 00:21:00.253
And we're excited about
that, because right now those

00:21:00.253 --> 00:21:02.420
are Windows devices that
are not do-- they're really

00:21:02.420 --> 00:21:04.760
sort of like a kiosk.

00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:07.550
They're not actually using the
underlying operating system

00:21:07.550 --> 00:21:08.580
on that device.

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:11.680
So there's really no reason
to have that out there

00:21:11.680 --> 00:21:13.220
and have to worry
about patching it

00:21:13.220 --> 00:21:15.390
for security and all those kind
of challenges that we have.

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:17.030
So putting the Chrome
devices out there,

00:21:17.030 --> 00:21:18.488
we feel very
confident that's going

00:21:18.488 --> 00:21:21.242
to give us a nice secure tool
out there for our clinicians.

00:21:21.242 --> 00:21:23.700
It's not going to get in the
way of what they do every day.

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:25.367
And it's going to be
a lot easier for IT

00:21:25.367 --> 00:21:28.700
to manage than just
the normal IT headaches

00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:31.810
that you have managing
a lot of devices.

00:21:31.810 --> 00:21:35.870
DONNA CHORNAWKA: Yeah,
very similar to continue

00:21:35.870 --> 00:21:41.310
to roll out Pixelbooks and
replace our Window environment

00:21:41.310 --> 00:21:43.940
and the laptops out
there right now.

00:21:43.940 --> 00:21:48.380
And again, just
showcasing and advocating

00:21:48.380 --> 00:21:53.390
how easy a Pixelbook is to
use and just helping people

00:21:53.390 --> 00:21:54.470
adopt that.

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:57.380
So again, continuing
to roll out Pixelbooks

00:21:57.380 --> 00:21:59.152
within our environment.

00:21:59.152 --> 00:22:00.200
DIANA CHONG: And Josh.

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:01.742
JOSHUA CHADD: Yeah,
and for us, we're

00:22:01.742 --> 00:22:04.010
really just looking at
refining the overall experience

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:07.820
on our very large
fleet that's out there.

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:10.952
So really just making it
maybe a little bit quicker,

00:22:10.952 --> 00:22:13.160
seeing what other use cases
within the store it makes

00:22:13.160 --> 00:22:15.350
sense to put on there,
and different workloads,

00:22:15.350 --> 00:22:17.420
as well as just making
access into that

00:22:17.420 --> 00:22:21.920
faster for specific
apps or functions

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:24.170
that the stores do today.

00:22:24.170 --> 00:22:27.530
And just overall making it a
really easy experience for them

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:29.932
and just continue
on that journey.

00:22:29.932 --> 00:22:30.890
DIANA CHONG: Excellent.

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:35.540
And then for those members
of the audience that are here

00:22:35.540 --> 00:22:38.960
learning about Chromebooks
for the first time, what kind

00:22:38.960 --> 00:22:41.840
of advice do you guys have for
customers who are evaluating it

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:44.720
for the first time?

00:22:44.720 --> 00:22:48.720
JASON TAHANEY: I would say
it's very easy to deploy.

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:51.390
Your user bases-- the training--
you don't really need that much

00:22:51.390 --> 00:22:54.000
training and education and we
thought we did and we found

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:56.520
that we didn't.

00:22:56.520 --> 00:22:59.810
And for us, it's a
modern operating system,

00:22:59.810 --> 00:23:03.053
so anytime we can do that in
health care is a good thing.

00:23:03.053 --> 00:23:05.220
So depending on your industry,
I would just jump in.

00:23:05.220 --> 00:23:06.012
I would just do it.

00:23:06.012 --> 00:23:09.330
It's worked out
very well for us.

00:23:09.330 --> 00:23:11.310
DONNA CHORNAWKA: Yeah,
I would echo that.

00:23:11.310 --> 00:23:12.800
I would definitely support it.

00:23:12.800 --> 00:23:15.950
If you would have seen the
keynote speaker this morning,

00:23:15.950 --> 00:23:17.750
